# libsecret -- the client library for the Secret Service D-Bus API.
#
# The thing applications call to store a password. gnome-keyring is the daemon
# that implements the API; this is what everything else links to talk to it,
# and gcr sits alongside both. GNOME Online Accounts, the Wi-Fi panel storing a
# PSK and any application with a "remember my password" box go through it.
#
# Not an LFS package, so it is pinned here rather than in versions.env.
# BLFS 12.4 pins 0.21.7.
SRC_URL=https://download.gnome.org/sources/libsecret/0.21/libsecret-0.21.7.tar.xz
SRC_SHA256=6b452e4750590a2b5617adc40026f28d2f4903de15f1250e1d1c40bfd68ed55e
SRC_DIR=libsecret-0.21.7

# ==============================================================================
# THREE DEFAULTS THAT DO NOT BUILD HERE, AND THE BOOK ONLY DISABLES ONE
# ==============================================================================
# BLFS configures this as `meson setup --prefix=/usr --buildtype=release
# -D gtk_doc=false ..` -- one switch. That works in BLFS because BLFS has Vala
# and docbook-xsl installed as recommended dependencies. This distribution has
# neither, so two more defaults have to be turned off, and both of them are
# `true` rather than `auto`:
#
# -Dmanpage=false. DEFAULTS TRUE, and it is the same trap upower has.
# docs/man/meson.build calls find_program('xsltproc') with NO required: false,
# then runs it against
#
#     http://docbook.sourceforge.net/release/xsl/current/manpages/docbook.xsl
#
# with --nonet. libxslt IS packaged, so xsltproc is found and the program check
# passes; the build then dies inside the XSLT because the DocBook stylesheets
# are not packaged and --nonet forbids fetching them. The failure names a
# stylesheet URL rather than an option.
#
# -Dvapi=false. DEFAULTS TRUE, and calls gnome.generate_vapi(), which needs
# vapigen from vala. vala is not packaged. This is the one that fails on a
# missing PROGRAM rather than a missing library.
#
# -Dgtk_doc=false. Defaults true and wants gi-docgen, which is not packaged.
# The one switch the book does pass.
#
# ==============================================================================
# -Dcrypto=libgcrypt, PINNED RATHER THAN LEFT AT ITS DEFAULT
# ==============================================================================
# The option is a combo -- libgcrypt, gnutls or disabled -- and libgcrypt is
# already the upstream default, so this changes nothing today. It is named
# because BOTH backends are now packaged and published in this tree, which
# makes the choice a real one rather than a foregone conclusion, and because a
# combo default is exactly the kind of thing that moves between releases.
#
# THE ARGUMENT IS ABOUT THE STACK, NOT THE LIBRARY. libsecret, gcr and
# gnome-keyring are one keyring stack, and gcr-4 requires libgcrypt outright --
# it is a hard dependency on its BLFS page, not a choice. Selecting gnutls here
# would put TWO crypto implementations inside one stack for no gain: libgcrypt
# would still be pulled in by gcr, and libsecret's transport encryption would
# be the odd one out. Same backend across the stack, stated in the recipe, and
# asserted as DT_NEEDED in install.sh.
#
# What it actually encrypts: the transport between an application and the
# keyring daemon over the session bus, so that a secret in flight is not
# readable by anything that can see the bus traffic. 'disabled' is a real
# choice upstream offers and would send passwords over D-Bus in the clear.
#
# ==============================================================================
# THE REST
# ==============================================================================
# -Dintrospection=true is already the default and is kept deliberately: gcr,
# gnome-keyring and every JavaScript consumer in the shell reach libsecret
# through Secret-1, and a build without the typelib installs and links fine.
#
# -Dbash_completion=disabled. 'auto', and bash IS packaged, so it would install
# a completion for secret-tool into a directory nothing on a running Duct
# system reads.
#
# NOT PASSED: -Dpam and -Dtpm2, both already false. The PAM module unlocks the
# keyring at login and is a gnome-keyring integration decision rather than a
# libsecret one; it is recorded here because "the keyring does not unlock at
# login" is a plausible future complaint and this is the switch behind it.
MESON_ARGS="-Dcrypto=libgcrypt -Dmanpage=false -Dvapi=false -Dgtk_doc=false \
-Dintrospection=true -Dbash_completion=disabled"
